Subject: JSON.parse takes each key from the shape being built or a per-parse key cache, and the three literals as predefined atoms

What this does
  - `JSParseState.json_key_hint`: before reading a key, the object parser passes the atom the depth's shape cache expects at that position (`json_next_key_token_hint`); `json_parse_key_string` first matches the input against that atom's bytes in one pass (each byte printable ASCII, no quote or backslash, then the closing quote) and takes the atom with no scan, hash or table lookup
  - otherwise the key's bytes are hashed while scanned and looked up in `JSParseState.json_kcache` (256 atoms of this parse, each holding a count, checked byte for byte; freed with the shape cache), and only a miss goes to JS_NewAtomLen
  - `true`, `false` and `null` followed by a byte that cannot continue an identifier are their predefined atoms, without the identifier scan and the atom table

Why we need it
  Key and literal interning (__JS_FindAtom, JS_NewAtomLen, __JS_FreeAtom, memcmp) was 17.8% of parse cycles on the app-traffic benchmark. json_app parse only: -6.9% cycles, -8.4% instructions (after 0581).

Soundness
  A key is taken from a hint or the cache only when the input bytes are exactly that atom's string and each of them is a byte the general key scan accepts unescaped, so the atom is the one JS_NewAtomLen would return for the same bytes; anything else goes the general way. The literals are matched on the same bytes json_parse_ident would accept, and the token is the same. Differential, old engine vs new, 160,000 inputs (two seeds; random nested values, arrays of objects with repeated and varied keys among id/name/x/__proto__/a"b/café/"1"/ab/abc/"", duplicate keys, escaped keys, numeric keys, surrogates, -0, denormal and overflowing numbers, near-literals such as trueX/nul; half mutated) give identical results and error messages.

